Revision history for Unblock-HTTP1

0.001  Development
    - Initial portable HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1 protocol engine.
    - Event-loop, transport, and operating-system neutral byte interface.
    - Uniform::HTTP 0.04 request and response objects.
    - Strict request and response parsing with picohttpparser.
    - Fixed-length, chunked, and close-delimited body framing.
    - Lossless HTTP/1 trailer delivery into Uniform::HTTP messages.
    - Streaming request and response bodies with cooperative backpressure.
    - Informational responses and exact 101/CONNECT protocol handoff boundary.
    - HTTP/1 parity pass for CONNECT and Upgrade validation.
    - RFC-correct HEAD, 1xx, 204, 205, and 304 response framing.
    - Expect: 100-continue handling before application body delivery.
    - HTTP/1.0 persistence and close-delimited streaming parity.
    - EOF truncation, cancellation, early-final-response, and backpressure tests.
    - Expanded framing, fragmentation, queue/reuse, and configured-limit tests.
    - Cross-platform CI on Linux, macOS, and Windows with Perl 5.16 coverage.
    - Standalone parse, serialization, fixed-body, chunked-body, and loopback
      benchmarks for optimization work.
    - Fast paths for common HTTP/1.1 request and response serialization plans.
    - Buffered HTTP/1.1 planning fast paths benchmark at roughly four times the
      full validation-path controls in same-run development measurements.
    - Bodyless and fully buffered fixed-length receive fast paths avoid
      unnecessary receive-state allocation while preserving callback lifecycle.
    - Focused fast-path regressions compare coalesced and fragmented body input.
    - Cross-engine receive benchmarks isolate Uniform message construction as
      the clearest remaining receive-side performance cost.
    - Callback and message-finalization diagnostics show those lifecycle calls
      are not useful optimization targets.
    - Added an explicit distribution MANIFEST and CI disttest coverage.
    - Documented the portable Linux::Event adapter and optional native bridge
      boundary without introducing Linux-specific engine code.
